the attacks in Paris last November  represent a frightening new phase in  terrorism across Europe  the threat is growing as is the web of  globally connected terrorists that is  the view of the director of Europol well  I think Paris showed that Isis in  particular have decided to go global  with their strategy to take on the West  using international network of  terrorists rather than just so-called  lone actors to therefore plan and to  carry out more spectacular style attacks  a picture that I see across Europe I  think it is likely that we will have  another attack we're working of course  round the clock to to prevent that from  happening but this is a very very  serious threat the Paris attackers were  Europeans radicalized in Europe trained  in Syria the number of people who pose a  threat to us all is according to shared  European intelligence huge there's at  least 5,000 we think that have been  radicalized by Isis very often online  and have acquired conflict experience in  Syria Iraq many of them have since  returned now there are groups of these  extremists that are meeting in Europe  and of course in Syria at locations that  we can't disclose but the threat is  increasing would you would you agree  with that it's certainly the highest  terrorist threat we've faced for over 10  years and you know we need to up our  game as a collective kanda terrorist  community to make sure that we can  protect our citizens from this the trail  from the Paris attacks led to terror  cells in Belgium it exposed intelligence  failings and highlighted gaps in  European Intelligence Sharing Europol  should have plugged those gaps mr.  Wainwright  a former mi5 officer says the  organisation's cooperation with over 600  law enforcement agencies across the  continent is now better than ever  but if Britain was to leave the EU he  says it would be disadvantaged and  exposed I think it gives rise to  significant security challenges at least  in the short term and you know I'm not  certain what those alternative  arrangements could be and indeed if  there were arrangements out there that  were better than  those that the current the UK's  currently choosing to engage in the EU  and assume they would have taken them  already so what one can expect that the  arrangements will be not as optimal and  therefore it will be difficult I think  for the UK to replace what it currently  relies on in the EU with arrangements  that will be as effective and as cost  cost-efficient as well the terror threat  is evolving fast he says often shaped in  the shadows of cyberspace and global in  its reach Mark Stone Sky News in The  Hague
 

